    <h2 class="font-mono text-accent text-xs tracking-widest uppercase mb-10">About</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-5 gap-12">
      <div class="md:col-span-3 space-y-5 text-ink leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I started in automotive engineering: an apprenticeship that began at Garringtons in 1989 and
          finished at Rover Group, then a decade at Rover Group working on ECU diagnostics, CAN bus systems, and vehicle test
          infrastructure. That background isn't incidental history. It means I think about software
          the way embedded engineers think about hardware, with a bias toward correctness over
          cleverness, and a habit of asking what happens when things go wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pivot to full-time software was gradual, accelerated by a First Class BSc in
          Computing and Electronics completed on day release while still at Rover Group. By the time I
          joined Omitec in 2006, the work was purely software: designing the vehicle diagnostic
          communications framework used by Tesla, Lotus, and Land Rover. I've been building
          production .NET systems ever since, through my own consultancy (Who Solutions Ltd,
          2010–2017) and for almost a decade as a senior engineer at a UK company that builds
          websites and .NET identity products, where I was involved in almost every project it
          released, built most of its CI/CD infrastructure, and specialised in DevOps,
          cross-platform development and identity, picking up a keen interest in AI integration
          as it arrived.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also build for myself. A Rust CLI for managing remote Ollama instances.
          A C# implementation of the Stanford ACE context engineering paper: three-agent architecture,
          .NET Aspire orchestration, PostgreSQL persistence. An MCP server for FreeAgent. And a public
          library of Claude Skills, reusable capability packages for Claude Code and Claude Desktop
          covering everything from Azure deployments to markdown formatting. If I recommend something,
          I've run it in production first.
        </p>
